WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:03.940
Carl Weathers sadly passed away on February
1.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:09.615
Few performers had the kind of range the actor
exhibited across dozens of film and television projects.

00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:14.710
Upon the news of his death, social media was
filled with his collaborators and fans mourning

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.209
the beloved actor.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:21.109
With roles in the Rocky movies and "Predator,"
Carl Weathers was probably seen as more of

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:24.140
an action or dramatic actor to much of the
general public.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:30.170
However, he proved just how hilarious he could
be with a starring turn in 1996's "Happy Gilmore."

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:34.910
He plays Chubbs, a former golfer who coaches
Happy to utilize his golfing prowess to the

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:38.385
fullest extent, waving a fake hand around
all the while.

00:00:38.385 --> 00:00:42.057
"Think of a place that you believe perfect.

00:00:42.057 --> 00:00:44.177
Your own happy place!"

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:48.442
Sandler took to Instagram to express his sorrow
at Weathers' passing, reflecting on what a

00:00:48.442 --> 00:00:52.680
great guy he was all around beyond simply
being a tremendous actor.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.000
"A true great man.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:54.617
Great dad.

00:00:54.617 --> 00:00:55.312
Great actor.

00:00:55.312 --> 00:00:56.312
Great athlete.

00:00:56.312 --> 00:00:58.160
So much fun to be around always.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.001
Smart as hell.

00:00:59.001 --> 00:00:59.816
Loyal as hell.

00:00:59.816 --> 00:01:00.906
Funny as hell.

00:01:00.906 --> 00:01:02.775
Loved his sons more than anything.

00:01:02.775 --> 00:01:04.255
What a guy!!"

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.820
Carl Weathers' most recent role prior to his
passing was Greef Karga on "The Mandalorian."

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.110
It's a role he occupied since the very first
episode, and he continued to appear throughout

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:16.890
its run as recently as Season 3.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:21.790
Karga became one of Din Djarin's closest allies,
and Weathers even directed a couple of episodes

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.250
of the Star Wars series.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:27.299
He's been a bright spot in the franchise,
and on Instagram, Pascal remembers his friend

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:30.760
with a quick statement that sums up the actor's
death:

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:31.920
"Words fail."

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.950
By the sound of it, Weathers had big plans
in mind for continuing Karga's journey.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:40.720
In an interview with Hypebeast, Weathers detailed
what he foresaw as the future of the character:

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.500
"I can see him not only protecting Nevarro
but becoming the tip of the spear in guarding

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:46.500
the planet.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.390
If there are any entities that want to come
in and take over, I don't think Greef Karga

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:55.280
is going to be a shy guy in stepping out front
and putting on his battle gear."

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:00.799
Ahmed Best faced a great deal of unfair criticism
after playing Jar Jar Binks in the Star Wars

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:01.799
prequels.

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:06.659
In 2023, Best returned to Star Wars, playing
Kelleran Beq in "The Foundling," an episode

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:09.619
of "The Mandalorian" that was directed by
Carl Weathers.

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:14.459
On Instagram, Best posted a beautiful statement
on Carl Weathers, including a picture of the

00:02:14.459 --> 00:02:15.750
two of them smiling.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.090
Best mentions looking up to Weathers ever
since he was a kid.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:22.410
Beyond that, Best reflected on how Weathers
helped him return to Star Wars:

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:27.060
"He saw how nervous I was to be back in #starwars
and gave me the strength and confidence to

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.620
perform."

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.620
Legacy sequels are always a risky proposition,
but "Creed" defied the odds and launched a

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.200
whole new franchise from where the Rocky films
left off.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.450
The film stars Michael B. Jordan as Adonis
Creed, son of Apollo Creed, whom Carl Weathers

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.178
played in several movies.

00:02:43.178 --> 00:02:46.678
Weathers wasn't in any of the Creed movies,
but his presence could be felt.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.760
It only makes sense that Jordan would honor
the man whose footsteps he walks in.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.480
On his Instagram stories, Jordan posted a
picture of Weathers along with the statement:

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.190
"We lost a legend."

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:00.480
Weathers may not have appeared alongside Jordan
in the Creed films, but he loved what the

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.480
actor did with the character.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.840
Shortly after the release of the first "Creed"
movie, Weathers posted on X, formerly known

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:06.628
as Twitter,

00:03:06.628 --> 00:03:09.250
"@michaelb4jordan You did well for the CREED
name!

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.050
Congrats on [a] great job, my friend!

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:13.420
May your success continue."

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:17.370
It's safe to say Jon Favreau respected Carl
Weathers on another level, as they were both

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.190
actors who eventually made their way to director's
chairs.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.780
Additionally, the two worked together on "The
Mandalorian."

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:27.140
Favreau's statement on Weathers' passing was
posted to StarWars.com, and it honors his

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.355
lengthy legacy.

00:03:28.355 --> 00:03:29.355
Favreau wrote,

00:03:29.355 --> 00:03:31.730
"I am heartbroken by this devastating loss.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:36.010
He was a hero from my childhood who I was
lucky enough to meet and then had the amazing

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.569
good fortune to work with.

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:42.010
He had the energy and curiosity of a young
man paired with the wisdom that his rich life

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:43.590
and career afforded him.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.709
My heart goes out to his family and countless
admirers."

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:52.409
"Dillon, you son of a b----!"

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:57.090
With that one statement in "Predator" between
Dutch and Dillon, as well as two manly hands

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:58.920
clasping, a meme was born.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.050
And the interaction might as well have really
summed up the friendship between the two actors,

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:06.980
as Schwarzenegger reflected on X on losing
such an incredible performer.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:11.776
Schwarzenegger wrote about all Weathers managed
to accomplish throughout an extraordinary career.

00:04:11.776 --> 00:04:15.788
Additionally, he wrote how he helped make
"Predator" the influential film it became.

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:16.550
He wrote,

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:18.320
"We couldn't have made Predator without him.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.041
And we certainly wouldn't have had such a
wonderful time making it."

00:04:21.041 --> 00:04:26.208
It appears the feeling was mutual, as Weathers
told Red Carpet News TV in 2016,

00:04:26.208 --> 00:04:27.729
"We had a lot of fun, too.

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:30.000
Lot of great guys on that movie."

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.070
Carl Weathers' comedic sensibilities in "Happy
Gilmore" shouldn't have been a surprise to

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:38.920
anyone who watched him host "Saturday Night
Live" on January 30, 1988.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:43.160
Weathers starred in an array of sketches,
including an installment of "Master Thespian,"

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.660
a recurring character played by Jon Lovitz.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:49.890
The show may have happened over 35 years ago,
but Lovitz still remembers what an astounding

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.470
performer Weathers was.

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:55.340
On X, Lovitz reflected on getting to work
with Weathers all those years ago while also

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.985
reflecting on how spectacular he was in "Rocky."

00:04:57.985 --> 00:05:02.410
Weathers' comedic timing would be on full
display over the years, as he would play himself

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:06.440
on "Arrested Development" and voiced the Basketball
King on "Regular Show."

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:08.630
He truly could do it all.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:13.139
Jones referred to Weathers as "Apollo Creed"
in a post to X while posting a picture of

00:05:13.139 --> 00:05:15.168
his character, Dillon, in "Predator."

00:05:15.168 --> 00:05:19.690
Another comedian, Dane Cook, offered a tribute
on X to Weathers, also pointing toward his

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:22.419
performance as Apollo Creed as one for the
ages.

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:26.370
Weathers may be gone, but his influence will
remain through an astonishingly excellent

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:27.450
body of work.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:31.750
Many of his colleagues will continue to remember
him, and that holds true for numerous fans

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:33.718
who cherish Weathers' movies.

